s, they explicitly state what needs to be proven. So for this theorem, we need to prove:\n\n- The bases are equal\n- The remaining angles are equal\n\nBecause the congruence of the whole triangle including all angles and sides can be derived from these.\n\nSo here's the complete formal statement:\n\n```tactics\ntheorem example_thm (a b c d e f : Point) (AB BC AC DE EF DF : Line) \n  (h1 : formTriangle a b c AB BC AC) \n  (h2 : formTriangle d e f DE EF DF) \n  (h3 : |(a─b)| = |(d─e)|) \n  (h4 : |(a─c)| = |(d─f)|) \n  (h5 : ∠ b:a:c = ∠ e:d:f) : \n  |(b─c)| = |(e─f)| ∧ \n  (∠ a:b:c = ∠ d:e:f) ∧ \n  (∠ a:c:b = ∠ d:f:e) := by \n```\n\nNow, let's verify this.\n\nWe have two triangles: ABC and DEF.\n\nHypotheses:\n- AB (|(a─b)|) = DE (|(d─e)|)\n- AC (|(a─c)|) = DF (|(d─f)|)\n- Angle at A (∠ b:a:c) = angle at D (∠ e:d:f)\n\nConclusions:\n- Base BC (|(b─c)|) = base EF (|(e─f)|)\n- Angle at B (∠ a:b:c) = angle at E (∠ d:e:f)\n- Angle at C (∠ a:c:b) = angle at F (∠ d:f:e)\n\nThis correctly formalizes the theorem.\n</think>\n```lean4\ntheorem example_thm (a b c d e f : Point) (AB BC AC DE EF DF : Line) \n  (h1 : formTriangle a b c AB BC AC) \n  (h2 : formTriangle d e f DE EF DF) \n  (h3 : |(a─b)| = |(d─e)|) \n  (h4 : |(a─c)| = |(d─f)|) \n  (h5 : ∠ b:a:c = ∠ e:d:f) : \n  |(b─c)| = |(e─f)| ∧ \n  (∠ a:b:c = ∠ d:e:f) ∧ \n  (∠ a:c:b = ∠ d:f:e) := by \n  sorry\n```": 0.16505643962142658, "theorem example_thm (a b c d e f : Point) (AB BC AC DE EF DF : Line) (h1 : formTriangle a b c AB BC AC) (h2 : formTriangle d e f DE EF DF) (h3 : |(a─b)| = |(d─e)|) (h4 : |(a─c)| = |(d─f)|) (h5 : (∠ a:b:c : ℝ) = ∠ d:e:f) : congruent (Triangle.ofPoints a b c) (Triangle.ofPoints d e f) := by sorry\n": 0.16469017152179163, "<think>\n# Formalizing the \"SAS\" Triangle Congruence Theorem in Lean 4\n\nThe given English statement is a classic result from Euclidean geometry known as the \"Side-Angle-Side\" (SAS) triangle congruence theorem. It states that if two triangles have two sides equal to two sides respectively and the included angles equal, then the triangles are congruent.\n\nLet's break down the theorem statement and understand what needs to be formalized:\n\n1. We have two triangles, ABC and DEF.\n2. The sides AB = DE and AC = DF (two sides equal to two sides respectively).\n3. The included angle BAC = angle EDF (the angle enclosed by the equal sides is equal).\n4. From this, the theorem concludes:\n   - The bases BC = EF.\n   - The triangles ABC and DEF are equal (congruent).\n   - The remaining angles are equal: angle ABC = angle DEF and angle ACB = angle DFE.\n\nIn the context of the Lean formalization, we need to:\n1. Define the theorem with the given conditions.\n2. Express the conclusion.\n3. Ensure all hypotheses and the conclusion align with the provided formal Lean types and structures.\n\nLet's examine the relevant parts of the theorem structure:\n\n```tactics\ntheorem example_thm (a b c d e f : Point) (AB DE AC DF BC EF : Line) (h1 : |(a─b)| = |(d─e)|) (h2 : |(a─c)| = |(d─f)|) (h3 : ∠ a:b:c = ∠ d:e:f) : (|(b─c)| = |(e─f)|) ∧ (Triangle.congruent (Triangle.ofPoints a b c) (Triangle.ofPoints d e f)) ∧ (∠ a:c:b = ∠ d:f:e) ∧ (∠ b:c:a = ∠ e:f:d) := by sorry\n```\n\nSo here:\n- a, b, c are the points of the first triangle.\n- d, e, f are the points of the second triangle.\n- AB, AC, BC are the lines of the first triangle.\n- DE, DF, EF are the lines of the second triangle.\n- The hypotheses state:\n  - |(a─b)| = |(d─e)|: side AB equals side DE.\n  - |(a─c)| = |(d─f)|: side AC equals side DF.\n  - ∠ a:b:c = ∠ d:e:f: angle BAC equals angle EDF (the angle at the included side).\n- The conclusion is that:\n  - |(b─c)| = |(e─f)|: side BC equals side EF.\n  - Triangle.congruent (Triangle.ofPoints a b c) (Triangle.ofPoints d e f): the two triangles are congruent.\n  - ∠ a:c:b = ∠ d:f:e: angle ACB equals angle DFE.\n  - ∠ b:c:a = ∠ e:f:d: angle ABC equals angle EFD.\n\nThe theorem signature and statements align precisely with the classic SAS congruence theorem.\n</think>\n```lean4\ntheorem example_thm (a b c d e f : Point) (AB DE AC DF BC EF : Line) (h1 : |(a─b)| = |(d─e)|) (h2 : |(a─c)| = |(d─f)|) (h3 : ∠ a:b:c = ∠ d:e:f) : (|(b─c)| = |(e─f)|) ∧ (Triangle.congruent (Triangle.ofPoints a b c) (Triangle.ofPoints d e f)) ∧ (∠ a:c:b = ∠ d:f:e) ∧ (∠ b:c:a = ∠ e:f:d) := by sorry\n```": 0.16456393695296603, "```tactics\ntheorem example_thm (a b c d e f : Point) (AB BC AC DE EF DF : Line) (h1 : formTriangle a b c AB BC AC) (h2 : formTriangle d e f DE EF DF) (h3 : |(a─b)| = |(d─e)|) (h4 : |(a─c)| = |(d─f)|) (h5 : (∠ a:b:c : ℝ) = ∠ d:e:f) : |(b─c)| = |(e─f)| ∧ congruent (△ a:b:c) (△ d:e:f) ∧ (∠ b:c:a = ∠ e:f:d) ∧ (∠ c:a:b = ∠ f:d:e) := by sorry\n```": 0.1643638659191944}, "cleaned_response": "∀ (a : Point) (b : Point) (c : Point) (d : Point) (e : Point) (f : Point) (AB : Line) (DE : Line) (AC : Line) (DF : Line) (BC : Line) (EF : Line), ((|(a─b)| = |(d─e)|) ∧ (|(a─c)| = |(d─f)|) ∧ (∠ a:b:c = ∠ d:e:f)) → ((|(b─c)| = |(e─f)|) ∧ (Triangle.congruent (Triangle.ofPoints a b c) (Triangle.ofPoints d e f)) ∧ (∠ a:c:b = ∠ d:f:e) ∧ (∠ b:c:a = ∠ e:f:d))", "prediction": {"∀ (a : Point) (b : Point) (c : Point) (d : Point) (e : Point) (f : Point) (AB : Line) (DE : Line) (AC : Line) (DF : Line) (BC : Line) (EF : Line), ((|(a─b)| = |(d─e)|) ∧ (|(a─c)| = |(d─f)|) ∧ (∠ a:b:c = ∠ d:e:f)) → ((|(b─c)| = |(e─f)|) ∧ (Triangle.congruent (Triangle.ofPoints a b c) (Triangle.ofPoints d e f)) ∧ (∠ a:c:b = ∠ d:f:e) ∧ (∠ b:c:a = ∠ e:f:d))": 1.0}, "groud_truth": "∀ (a b c d e f : Point) (AB BC AC DE EF DF : Line), formTriangle a b c AB BC AC ∧ formTriangle d e f DE EF DF ∧ |(a─b)| = |(d─e)| ∧ |(a─c)| = |(d─f)| ∧ (∠ b:a:c = ∠ e:d:f) → |(b─c)| = |(e─f)| ∧ (∠ a:b:c = ∠ d:e:f) ∧ (∠ a:c:b = ∠ d:f:e)"}